Stock Portfolio Tracker — Non-Functional Requirements
Status: Approved — streamlined MVP baseline
Technology: Java 21, Spring Boot, PostgreSQL, REST, gRPC
Scope and priorities
All 13 NFR identifiers are retained. MVP items are required for the first working release; Enhancements are planned after the application works; Future items are intentionally out of scope. Approval records a design decision, not proof of implementation. Numeric targets are goals until measured.

These NFRs do not change the approved functional requirements. In particular, preserve authenticated portfolio ownership; configurable concurrency-safe limits of 100 portfolios per user and 500 unique holdings per portfolio; supported U.S.-listed USD stocks/ETFs; fractional shares to six decimal places; weighted-average cost; immutable holding security identity; BigDecimal/HALF_EVEN calculations; and honest partial valuations. Pricing must distinguish provider observation time from local cache age, use an initial configurable 15-second intraday cache TTL, limit stale intraday fallback to a configurable initial five-minute observation age, and treat official closing prices separately after hours. The functional requirements document remains authoritative for detailed business rules.
NFR-001: API Performance
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: High

Description: Deliver responsive operations and baseline performance visibility without requiring extensive benchmarking before MVP.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. CRUD operations do not unnecessarily invoke the Pricing Service.
2. Valuations use the Pricing gRPC Service and eligible cached prices.
3. Database and outbound service operations use configurable timeouts/deadlines.
4. Basic REST latency and HTTP response status metrics are available.
5. Avoid obvious N+1 database queries and duplicate price fetches within a valuation.
6. Record representative baseline timings for CRUD, cached valuations, and provider-backed valuations.
Initial performance goals — not MVP release gates
Workload	p95 goal
Portfolio/holding CRUD	≤ 300 ms
Cached portfolio valuation	≤ 500 ms
Provider-dependent valuation	≤ 2 seconds


Measurements must identify workload, dataset, environment, and cache status.

Enhancements: k6/JMeter load testing at 20/50/100 concurrent virtual users; p50/p95/p99, throughput, errors, bottleneck tuning.
Future: Production SLOs, distributed load tests, automated performance gates.
NFR-002: Scalability
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: Medium

Description: Avoid barriers to growth without implementing multi-instance infrastructure initially.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. The REST API is stateless; no server-local user session is required.
2. Portfolio REST API and Pricing gRPC Service deploy independently.
3. Ports, service addresses, resource limits, and HikariCP pool settings are configurable.
4. Concurrent requests preserve NFR-006 data integrity.
5. The Pricing Service uses configurable caching and respects provider rate limits.
6. Document per-instance local-cache limitations for potential future replicas.
Enhancements: 20/50/100-user testing, pool utilization measurements, bounded parallel pricing, cache tradeoff analysis.
Future: Multiple replicas, load balancers, distributed cache, Kubernetes, autoscaling.
NFR-003: Reliability and Resilience
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: High

Description: Handle dependency failures without misleading users about market prices or valuation completeness.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Configure HTTP timeouts and gRPC deadlines to avoid indefinite waits.
2. Use bounded retries for eligible transient/idempotent calls only (initial maximum two retries, with backoff); do not retry invalid symbols or validation failures.
3. Distinguish provider quote observation age from local cache age and expose fresh/delayed/stale status.
4. On provider outage during market hours, allow stale intraday prices only within the configured observation-age maximum (initially five minutes), clearly marked stale.
5. Use valid official closing prices under separate after-hours eligibility rules.
6. Return unavailable-price details and partial valuations rather than fabricated complete totals.
7. Map dependency failures to meaningful errors and log them without secrets.
8. Bound retries and outbound requests to respect provider quotas.
Enhancements: Resilience4j circuit breakers, fallback metrics, outage/recovery tests, retry tuning.
Future: Multi-provider failover and chaos engineering infrastructure.
NFR-004: Security
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: High

Description: Protect portfolios, tokens, credentials, and service interfaces.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Validate JWT signature, issuer, audience, and expiration using Spring Security OAuth2 Resource Server.
2. Require authentication and enforce portfolio/holding ownership at service and data-access boundaries.
3. Never accept user-supplied ownership as authorization.
4. Validate inputs and avoid leaking secrets or stack traces in errors/logs.
5. Externalize secrets; do not commit them to Git or bake them into container images.
6. Use least-privilege database/provider credentials.
7. Use TLS for remotely deployed services; isolated local development may use HTTP.
8. Test invalid/missing tokens and cross-user access attempts.
Enhancements: Dependency scanning and deeper security tests.
Future: Custom identity infrastructure and enterprise policy/secrets platforms.
NFR-005: Observability and Monitoring
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: Medium

Description: Make requests diagnosable without requiring a full monitoring stack on day one.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Produce consistent structured logs without sensitive tokens or API keys.
2. Propagate trace context (trace and span IDs) across REST, gRPC, and relevant outbound HTTP calls.
3. Expose basic health checks and Micrometer metrics for REST latency/errors and pricing/cache activity.
4. Document how to inspect local logs and correlation identifiers.
5. Avoid unbounded high-cardinality metrics labels such as user IDs or ticker symbols.
Enhancements: Jaeger/OpenTelemetry visualization, Prometheus/Grafana dashboards, detailed provider/cache/pool/circuit-breaker metrics.
Future: Centralized production alerting and enterprise telemetry platforms.
NFR-006: Data Integrity and Consistency
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: High

Description: Preserve ownership, financial correctness, and consistency under concurrent operations.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Use PostgreSQL, JPA, and transactions for multi-step writes.
2. Enforce ownership, foreign keys, required fields, and uniqueness through appropriate database constraints and application checks.
3. Use @Version or equivalent conflict protection for concurrent portfolio/holding modifications.
4. Enforce configurable 100-portfolio/user and 500-unique-holding/portfolio limits safely under concurrent creation.
5. Use BigDecimal and approved calculation/rounding rules; no floating-point financial arithmetic.
6. Preserve weighted-average cost rules and immutable holding security identity.
7. Apply versioned Flyway migrations.
8. Test rollback, uniqueness, concurrent updates, and capacity limits against PostgreSQL.
9. Avoid holding database transactions open while awaiting external pricing calls.
Enhancements: Isolation/deadlock experiments and deeper concurrency stress tests.
Future: Distributed transactions and event sourcing.
NFR-007: Testing and Quality Assurance
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: High

Description: Verify critical business behavior and service boundaries with a manageable test suite.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Unit-test valuation math, weighted-average cost, validation, price freshness, and partial results.
2. Integration-test REST behavior, JWT/ownership, persistence, and Flyway migrations.
3. Use PostgreSQL Testcontainers for database-specific integration behavior.
4. Test gRPC contracts/service behavior using an in-process server or equivalent.
5. Stub external market data; CI must not depend on a live provider.
6. Test important failure paths, timeouts, and concurrent write conflicts.
7. Provide a documented Maven test command and deterministic core suite.
Enhancements: JaCoCo and an ~80% core business-logic coverage target (not a release gate), broader WireMock/resilience/load suites.
Future: Enterprise contract testing and large test environments.
NFR-008: Deployment and Portability
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: Medium

Description: Run locally and reproducibly without requiring paid cloud infrastructure.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Build both Java 21 services using Maven.
2. Containerize both application services.
3. Provide Docker Compose for the services and PostgreSQL.
4. Persist PostgreSQL data with a named volume.
5. Document startup, migrations, configuration, and health checks.
6. Do not require AWS, Kubernetes, or paid monitoring for MVP.
7. Document any provider account/key prerequisites and verify provider pricing before claiming a free integration.
Enhancements: Optional local observability profile, CI pipeline, optional AWS deployment with cost controls.
Future: Kubernetes, managed infrastructure, production-grade deployment automation.
NFR-009: Maintainability and Code Quality
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: Medium

Description: Keep responsibilities clear and implementation understandable.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Separate transport/controllers, application services, domain, and persistence/integration code.
2. Portfolio API owns portfolios, holdings, authorization, and valuation; Pricing Service owns provider integration, price freshness, and caching.
3. Version OpenAPI and .proto contracts with the code.
4. Apply consistent validation, errors, naming, and configuration.
5. Document significant architectural tradeoffs briefly.
6. Maintain README build/run/test instructions.
7. Prefer small reviewable changes and avoid speculative abstractions.
Enhancements: Additional ADRs, static analysis, architecture tests.
Future: Complex plugin architectures and enterprise governance.
NFR-010: Availability and Recovery
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: Medium

Description: Support predictable local operation and data persistence without promising production uptime.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Expose health endpoints.
2. Keep PostgreSQL data persistent across container recreation.
3. Shut down cleanly and release managed resources.
4. Document local restart/recovery steps.
5. Document a basic pg_dump/pg_restore procedure; automation and a validated recovery drill are not MVP gates.
Enhancements: Backup/restore drill, restart/readiness tests, evaluation of 30-minute RTO and 24-hour RPO planning targets with an actual backup schedule.
Future: Formal SLAs, automated failover, disaster recovery and multi-region redundancy.

Note: A Docker volume alone does not provide a 24-hour RPO. Targets are not guarantees until tested.
NFR-011: Configuration and Environment Management
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: Medium

Description: Keep settings reproducible, validated, and separate from source code.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Use Spring YAML/profiles and environment-variable overrides.
2. Group related settings with validated @ConfigurationProperties.
3. Externalize DB, identity-provider, market-provider, service endpoint, timeout, cache TTL, maximum stale observation age, and pool settings.
4. Fail startup on invalid/missing required settings rather than unsafe silent defaults.
5. Include a sanitized .env.example and environment setup instructions.
6. Never commit or log secrets.
Enhancements: Broader configuration validation and tests.
Future: Central configuration and enterprise secrets infrastructure.
NFR-012: Resource Management and Concurrency
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: Medium

Description: Bound resource usage and protect concurrent operations without premature concurrency frameworks.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Use managed HikariCP connections and reuse gRPC channels and appropriate HTTP connections.
2. Limit concurrent provider calls and respect provider rate limits independently of thread counts.
3. Maintain correct simultaneous portfolio/holding updates and capacity enforcement (NFR-006).
4. Apply network deadlines/timeouts.
5. Manage and close application-owned executors and clients on shutdown.
6. Avoid uncontrolled thread creation, unbounded queues, and long DB transactions around network calls.
7. If parallel pricing is implemented in MVP, explicitly bound its concurrency and aggregate partial results safely.
Enhancements: CompletableFuture fan-out, executor metrics, concurrency benchmarks, virtual-thread vs platform-thread experiments.
Future: Adaptive concurrency, distributed scheduling, advanced backpressure.

Note: Virtual threads do not replace provider quotas, connection pools, or concurrency limits.
NFR-013: API Compatibility and Versioning
Status: Approved — MVP Scope | Priority: Medium

Description: Establish stable, versioned contracts without supporting multiple live versions initially.
MVP acceptance criteria
1. Version REST routes as /api/v1 and protobuf packages as pricing.v1 (or equivalent agreed names).
2. Document REST with OpenAPI and gRPC with version-controlled .proto files.
3. Use consistent validation, HTTP status codes, and error contracts.
4. Preserve protobuf field numbers/types during compatible evolution; reserve removed field numbers and names.
5. Review REST field removals/renames, new required fields, and semantic changes as potentially breaking.
6. Test important REST/gRPC contract behavior; never hand-edit generated code.
Enhancements: More automated compatibility tests and contract-change review.
Future: Multiple active versions, formal deprecation schedules, schema governance.
Delivery sequence
Phase	Focus	Exit condition
1 — Foundation	Maven modules, Docker Compose, PostgreSQL/Flyway, config, health	Both services start and migrations run
2 — Core domain	JWT ownership, portfolios, holdings, transactions, precision and limits	CRUD and domain tests pass
3 — Pricing integration	gRPC, provider adapter, cache, freshness, fallback	End-to-end complete/partial valuations work
4 — MVP quality	Tests, basic logs/traces/metrics, README, timing baseline	MVP criteria verified
5 — Enhancements	Circuit breakers, dashboards, load tests, parallel pricing, virtual threads	Each improvement measured and documented


MVP definition of done
- [ ] Both services build, start, and communicate locally.
- [ ] JWT authentication and ownership checks prevent cross-user access.
- [ ] Portfolio/holding business rules and concurrency-safe limits are enforced.
- [ ] Decimal calculations and partial/fresh/delayed/stale valuations are correct.
- [ ] Provider failures do not produce misleading complete valuations.
- [ ] Core unit and integration tests pass.
- [ ] Health, basic metrics, structured logs, and cross-service trace context work.
- [ ] Docker Compose and README work in a clean local setup.
- [ ] Baseline timings are recorded; unverified targets are not claimed as achieved.
- [ ] Deferred enhancements remain tracked separately.
Scope control
Favor a working end-to-end vertical slice over infrastructure built for hypothetical scale. Add enhancements when they solve an observed problem or meet a specific learning objective. Preserve approved functional business rules even when simplifying non-functional scope.